* diffprobe.lib -- differential / common-mode loop-gain probe (ngspice_augmentation,
* card #451 subtask 2.2.4, 2026-09-27)
*
* Spectre's diffprobe (research/cadence-stb-pstb.md §1) is two iprobe branches
* coupled by a vcvs/pcccs pair whose gain is altered to -1 (differential mode) or
* +1 (common mode) before running stb on the branch-1 probe. ngspice-45.2 can alter
* a device's acmag with `alter @dev[acmag]=...` (used throughout lgprobe.lib /
* tools/stb.py already) but there is no equally simple way to alter a live E/F
* source's gain parameter mid-run without `alterparam` + `reset`, which would
* throw away the operating point set up by the leading `op` and complicate the
* two-injection flow in tools/stb.run(). So this file takes the task's other
* offered option: two separate subcircuit variants, diffprobe_dm and diffprobe_cm,
* selected at deck-preparation time (see tools/stb.select_diff_mode()), not at
* simulation time.
*
* Structure: two lgprobe-like branches.
*   branch 1 (a1/b1): the read probe -- Ii1/Vi1/Vb1, identical to stb/lgprobe.lib.
*     tools/stb.py injects and reads ONLY this branch (v(p.x1), i(v.p.vi1),
*     i(v.p.vb1), v(a1)), with the same Tian formula as the single-ended case.
*   branch 2 (a2/b2): a second, independently-sourced but correlated branch. Its
*     Ii2/Vi2 are driven with the same acmag as branch 1's Ii1/Vi1 (tools/stb.py
*     alters both pairs together) but at a fixed AC phase baked in here: 0 deg
*     (in phase) for diffprobe_cm, 180 deg (antiphase) for diffprobe_dm. A source
*     at acmag=1 acphase=180 is exactly -1 * (acmag=1 acphase=0) in a linear AC
*     analysis, so this reproduces Spectre's "gain=-1 for DM, gain=+1 for CM"
*     without needing a live gain alter. `alter` only ever touches acmag here, so
*     the baked-in acphase survives every alter, exactly as branch 1's does.
*
* Why reading branch 1 alone is enough: for a network that is exactly symmetric
* between its p (branch-1-attached) and n (branch-2-attached) halves, a purely
* antisymmetric (DM) or purely symmetric (CM) excitation makes the whole
* network's response antisymmetric or symmetric too. T is a ratio of the same
* excitation on both sides of the fraction, so reading it at one symmetric point
* (branch 1) gives exactly the DM or CM loop gain of the whole coupled network --
* this is the standard differential/common-mode half-circuit theorem, and it is
* exact (not approximate) for an exactly symmetric linear circuit. See the
* stb_diff checks in tests/test_stb.py, which compare against the DM and CM
* half circuits built directly from that theorem.
*
* Both branches are a DC short (Vb1, Vb2 are 0 V), same as lgprobe, so the
* operating point is the closed-loop one on both wires.
*
* Orientation, per wire, matches lgprobe.lib: a = downstream (the side the loop
* signal travels to), b = upstream (the side it comes from). Both wires of a
* differential/CM loop must be broken with the same downstream/upstream sense
* for the DM and CM correlation (in-phase / antiphase) to mean what it says.
*
* A deck that instantiates the generic subckt name "diffprobe" (the marker
* tools/stb.select_diff_mode() rewrites) without going through tools/stb.py's
* --mode dm|cm will fail to elaborate: there is no subckt literally named
* "diffprobe" in this file, on purpose, so there is no silent default mode.

.subckt diffprobe_dm a1 b1 a2 b2
Ii1 0 x1 DC 0 AC 0 0
Vi1 x1 a1 DC 0 AC 1 0
Vb1 b1 x1 DC 0
Ii2 0 x2 DC 0 AC 0 180
Vi2 x2 a2 DC 0 AC 1 180
Vb2 b2 x2 DC 0
.ends diffprobe_dm

.subckt diffprobe_cm a1 b1 a2 b2
Ii1 0 x1 DC 0 AC 0 0
Vi1 x1 a1 DC 0 AC 1 0
Vb1 b1 x1 DC 0
Ii2 0 x2 DC 0 AC 0 0
Vi2 x2 a2 DC 0 AC 1 0
Vb2 b2 x2 DC 0
.ends diffprobe_cm
